TI  - Chronic Myeloid Leukemia Progressing to Early T-Cell Precursor Lymphoblastic 
      Blast Phase Following Treatment Default: An Integrated Morphologic, 
      Immunophenotypic, Cytogenetic, and Molecular Case Report.
PG  - e117154
LID - 10.7759/cureus.117154 [doi]
LID - e117154
AB  - Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is a BCR::ABL1-positive myeloid neoplasm 
      characterized by the Philadelphia chromosome resulting from t(9;22)(q34;q11.2). 
      Although tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) have substantially improved outcomes 
      in chronic-phase CML, progression to blast phase remains an important feature of 
      advanced disease. The majority of CML blast-phase cases demonstrate myeloid or 
      precursor B-cell differentiation, whereas T-lineage transformation is uncommon. 
      Early T-cell precursor acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ETP-ALL) represents an 
      immature T-lineage neoplasm characterized by expression of cytoplasmic CD3 and 
      CD7, weak or absent expression of conventional mature T-cell antigens, and 
      frequent expression of myeloid or stem-cell-associated markers. A 35-year-old man 
      with a known diagnosis of CML in chronic phase, receiving dasatinib and reported 
      to be a frequent treatment defaulter, presented with blast transformation. 
      Peripheral blood examination demonstrated marked leukocytosis, anemia, and 
      thrombocytopenia, with 64% circulating blasts. The blasts were large, with scant 
      cytoplasm and pleomorphic nuclei. Flow cytometric immunophenotyping identified an 
      abnormal blast population comprising approximately 82% of viable events. The 
      blasts expressed CD34, HLA-DR, CD123, cytoplasmic CD3, CD5 (dim), CD7, CD13, and 
      CD33, with variable expression of CD117 and CD56. They were negative for surface 
      CD3, CD1a, CD4, CD8, B-lineage markers, TCR-gammadelta, and myeloperoxidase. The overall 
      immunophenotype was consistent with ETP-ALL. Cytogenetic analysis demonstrated 
      47,XY,del(7)(q21),+8,t(9;22)(q34;q11.2), indicating the presence of the 
      Philadelphia chromosome together with trisomy 8 and del(7q). Molecular analysis 
      detected an ABL1 p.Phe317Leu (F317L) kinase-domain mutation. This case represents 
      a rare instance of CML blast phase with an ETP lymphoblastic phenotype and 
      demonstrates the coexistence of an ABL1 F317L kinase-domain mutation and 
      additional cytogenetic abnormalities, including trisomy 8 and del(7q). The case 
      emphasizes the importance of integrating the clinical history with morphologic, 
      flow cytometric, cytogenetic, and molecular findings when an immature T-cell 
      leukemia is identified in a patient with established CML.
