TI  - Extensive vertebral compression fractures as the initial manifestation of 
      childhood B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia despite limited peripheral blood 
      abnormalities: a case report.
PG  - 1945539
LID - 10.3389/fped.2026.1945539 [doi]
LID - 1945539
AB  - BACKGROUND: Musculoskeletal manifestations may appear before hematologic 
      abnormalities in childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), making diagnosis 
      difficult. Extensive vertebral compression fractures involving the cervical, 
      thoracic, and lumbar spine as the first sign of ALL are very rare, particularly 
      when peripheral blood abnormalities are limited and circulating blasts are 
      absent. CASE PRESENTATION: We report a 5-year-old boy who presented with severe 
      back pain and inability to walk after minor trauma. The initial lumbar computed 
      tomography (CT) was reported as showing no definite vertebral fracture, but 
      whole-spine magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) showed diffuse bone marrow 
      infiltration with extensive pathological compression fractures involving the 
      cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae. Peripheral blood tests showed 
      leukopenia and neutropenia without circulating blasts, which contributed to the 
      initial diagnostic uncertainty. Bone marrow examination confirmed common B-cell 
      acute lymphoblastic leukemia with high hyperdiploidy and a KRAS p.G13D mutation. 
      After induction therapy according to the South China Children's Cancer Group 
      (SCCCG)-ALL-2023 protocol, his back pain was greatly relieved, he regained 
      independent ambulation, and flow cytometric and molecular measurable residual 
      disease (MRD) assessments became negative. CONCLUSION: Extensive vertebral 
      compression fractures can be an early manifestation of childhood ALL, even when 
      circulating blasts are absent and peripheral blood abnormalities are limited. 
      Children with persistent axial skeletal pain or unexplained vertebral compression 
      fractures should be evaluated for hematologic malignancy, especially when their 
      symptoms are more severe than expected from routine imaging or peripheral blood 
      findings. Early whole-spine MRI and timely bone marrow examination may help 
      establish the diagnosis and avoid unnecessary delay.
