TI  - Ameliorative Effect of Lycopene and Capsaicinoid Glucoside in Acute Myeloid 
      Leukemia.
LID - e71162 [pii]
LID - 10.1002/jbt.71162 [doi]
AB  - Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a hematological malignancy with limited 
      therapeutic efficacy, particularly in elderly and chemotherapy-resistant 
      patients. Although cytarabine and daunorubicin are the conventional treatments, 
      their 5-year survival rate is less than 15% in some populations. Nonetheless, 
      natural bioactive substances with antileukemic properties might be a viable 
      treatment strategy. The antileukemic potential of lycopene (Lyc) and capsaicinoid 
      glucoside (CG), individually and in combination, is examined in this study as a 
      possible adjuvant or alternative treatment. This study employed both in-vitro 
      (HL-60 cell line) and in-vivo (benzene-induced leukemia rat model) methods. 
      Cytotoxicity, apoptosis, and proliferation were evaluated in-vitro using MTT 
      assays, Trypan blue, crystal violet staining and flow cytometry (Annexin-V/PI). 
      Hematological parameters (WBC, RBC, neutrophils), oxidative stress indicators 
      (SOD, GSH), qPCR and ELISA for anti-apoptotic/proliferative markers (Bcl-2, 
      NF-kappaB, Ki-67, PCNA) were assessed in in vivo tests. To verify morphological 
      alterations, blood smear analysis was carried out. Lyc and CG showed 
      dose-dependent cytotoxicity in vitro, synergistically Lyc+CG showed lowest 
      cytotoxicity with IC(50) values of 21.35. Synergistic effects were shown by the 
      Lyc+CG combination, which dramatically decreased cell viability and increased 
      apoptosis (40.59% early apoptosis, 18.72% late apoptosis). In-vitro and in-vivo 
      study showed that Lyc+CG reduced proliferation and anti-apoptotic effect, 
      restored SOD and GSH levels, and normalized haematological parameters 
      (p < 0.001). The Lyc+CG group's blood smears revealed more apoptotic bodies and 
      fewer blast cells. By causing apoptosis, lowering oxidative stress, and 
      preventing proliferation in cell line (HL-60) and animal models (benzene-induced 
      leukemia in rats), the combination of Lyc and CG demonstrates strong antileukemic 
      action. These results encourage more research into Lyc+CG as a possible AML 
      treatment approach. To completely clarify its therapeutic potential, future 
      research should concentrate on clinical trials and molecular insights.
