TI  - Recurrent Pleural Effusion as the Initial Presentation of T-lineage Lymphoblastic 
      Malignancy in a Child: A Diagnostic Challenge.
PG  - e116811
LID - 10.7759/cureus.116811 [doi]
LID - e116811
AB  - Pleural effusion in children is most frequently associated with infectious 
      diseases, particularly parapneumonic effusion. Recurrent or unexplained pleural 
      effusion, however, should prompt consideration of less common etiologies, 
      including autoimmune disease, tuberculosis, chylothorax, cardiac and renal 
      disease, and malignancy. We report the case of a 10-year-old boy with recurrent 
      large right-sided pleural effusions requiring repeated drainage of more than 
      1,500 mL. An extensive diagnostic evaluation for rheumatologic and infectious 
      causes was unrevealing. During the current admission, the patient had anorexia, 
      weight loss, axillary lymphadenopathy, leukocytosis, and mild thrombocytopenia, 
      without reported peripheral blood blasts. Chest computed tomography demonstrated 
      a large right pleural collection, bilateral axillary lymphadenopathy, and 
      prominent thymic tissue without a definite bulky anterior mediastinal mass. 
      Pleural-fluid flow cytometry demonstrated an abnormal immature T-lineage 
      lymphoblast population comprising approximately 99% of analyzed cells. Pleural 
      biopsy showed tissue infiltration by atypical lymphoid cells morphologically 
      resembling non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Subsequent bone marrow examination, performed 
      after three days of methylprednisolone therapy, demonstrated diffuse blast 
      infiltration with an immature T-cell immunophenotype and was diagnostic of T-cell 
      acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Lymph-node biopsy showed lymphoproliferative 
      disease, while lumbar puncture was normal. Pleural-fluid tuberculosis PCR and 
      acid-fast bacilli testing were negative. This case emphasizes that recurrent 
      pleural effusion may be an initial and predominant manifestation of T-cell acute 
      lymphoblastic leukemia, even in the absence of peripheral blood blasts or a 
      typical mediastinal mass. Early pleural-fluid flow cytometry may facilitate 
      diagnosis and prevent diagnostic delay.
