TI  - Atypical Extramedullary Acute Myeloid Leukemia in Infancy: Bilateral Testicular 
      Swelling and Scalp Lesions at Diagnosis.
PG  - 2024335
LID - 10.1155/crh/2024335 [doi]
LID - 2024335
AB  - Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is rare in infancy, with monocytic subtypes such as 
      acute myelomonocytic leukemia often demonstrating extramedullary involvement. 
      This report describes a 10-month-old male infant presenting with firm bilateral 
      testicular enlargement and multiple parietal scalp nodules, accompanied by 
      leukocytosis and circulating blasts in peripheral blood. Bone marrow examination 
      confirmed French-American-British (FAB) M4 AML. Scrotal ultrasound demonstrated 
      heterogeneous masses, and computed tomography identified scalp chloromas without 
      bone erosion. The patient received AML chemotherapy, resulting in resolution of 
      extramedullary lesions and achievement of remission. This case underscores the 
      importance of early hematologic evaluation in infants with bilateral testicular 
      masses to prevent misdiagnosis. It illustrates the rare occurrence of concurrent 
      testicular and scalp chloromas as the initial manifestation of infant AML.
