TI  - Diagnosis of acute myeloid leukaemia in a previously diagnosed case of chronic 
      lymphocytic leukaemia in a dog.
LID - 10.1111/avj.70153 [doi]
AB  - A 12-year-old female spayed Irish wolfhound presented with a 6-month history of 
      weight loss, chronic vomiting and diarrhoea and a persistent progressive 
      lymphocytosis with previously diagnosed CD8+ T-cell chronic lymphocytic leukaemia 
      (CLL). CLL was initially diagnosed through flow cytometry of EDTA-preserved 
      peripheral blood. The decision to treat was based on a progressive circulating 
      neoplastic lymphocytic population in the face of clinical deterioration. 
      Progressive clinical decline was identified in the several weeks after 
      commencement of treatment with chlorambucil and pegylated asparaginase for 
      progressive CLL. Repeated haemogram and blood smear were suggestive of a second 
      morphologically distinct circulating neoplastic population. Repeated flow 
      cytometry of peripheral blood was consistent with a concurrent acute myeloid 
      leukaemia (AML) to previously identified CD8+ CLL. Emergence of AML coincided 
      with development of upwards of five cutaneous lesions, with concern for leukaemia 
      cutis. High-dose chemotherapy with vincristine (0.7 mg/m(2)) was commenced. 
      Despite more aggressive treatment, rapid clinical deterioration ensued, and 
      euthanasia was elected. To the author's knowledge, this is the first report of 
      diagnosis of concurrent AML and CLL in a dog.
