TI  - Pancytopenia Misattributed to Parvovirus B19 Infection Revealing Acute 
      Promyelocytic Leukemia.
PG  - e115747
LID - 10.7759/cureus.115747 [doi]
LID - e115747
AB  - Pancytopenia is a diagnostic challenge due to its broad differential diagnosis, 
      including infections and hematological malignancies. Parvovirus B19 infection is 
      a recognized but uncommon cause of transient pancytopenia. However, positive 
      viral serology or viremia may lead to diagnostic anchoring and delay the 
      identification of underlying malignant disorders. We report the case of a 
      17-year-old previously healthy female presenting with persistent fever and 
      tonsillitis. Laboratory investigations revealed pancytopenia with 
      agranulocytosis. Blood serology and polymerase chain reaction (PCR) were positive 
      for Parvovirus B19. The peripheral blood smear was normal. An initial diagnosis 
      of virus-related pancytopenia was established. Despite supportive treatment, 
      including granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, transfusions, and intravenous 
      immunoglobulins, pancytopenia persisted. Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy were 
      performed. The bone marrow aspiration showed a hypercellular marrow without 
      abnormal cells by flow cytometry within the conventional blast gate. Subsequent 
      pathological examination of the biopsy revealed massive blast infiltration 
      consistent with acute promyelocytic leukemia. The patient rapidly developed 
      disseminated intravascular coagulation, and atypical promyelocytes later appeared 
      in the peripheral blood smear. Targeted treatment with all-trans retinoic acid 
      and arsenic trioxide was initiated, leading to remission. This case highlights 
      the risk of delayed diagnosis when pancytopenia is attributed to viral infection. 
      Persistent cytopenia, even in the presence of positive Parvovirus B19 testing and 
      a normal blood smear, should prompt early bone marrow examination to exclude 
      underlying hematological malignancy.
