TI  - Gelatinous transformation and concomitant fibrosis of bone marrow in a patient 
      with relapsed myelodysplastic syndrome following unrelated bone marrow 
      transplantation.
PG  - 102448
LID - S0966-3274(26)00106-1 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.trim.2026.102448 [doi]
AB  - Gelatinous transformation of bone marrow (GTBM) is a rare histological condition 
      characterized by deposition of hyaluronic acid-rich gelatinous material, usually 
      accompanied by adipocyte atrophy, reduced reticulin fibers, and hypocellularity. 
      Although most frequently associated with malnutrition and severe weight loss, 
      GTBM has also been reported in hematological malignancies. The coexistence of 
      GTBM and bone marrow fibrosis is rarely described, and its underlying mechanisms 
      remain unclear. We report a 69-year-old woman with myelodysplastic syndrome with 
      excess blasts-1 (MDS-EB-1) harboring a complex karyotype including chromosome 7 
      abnormalities, classified as very high risk by IPSS-R. She underwent unrelated 
      bone marrow transplantation and achieved complete remission. On day 35, she 
      developed acute skin graft-versus-host disease (GVHD), which resolved with 
      immunosuppressive therapy. Five months later, she presented with pancytopenia, 
      chronic cutaneous GVHD, severe anorexia, 15-kg weight loss, and malnutrition (BMI 
      11.5 kg/m(2)). Bone marrow aspiration yielded a dry tap, while biopsy revealed 
      Alcian blue-positive gelatinous material, reticulin fibrosis, and preserved 
      adipocytes. Her disease progressed to acute myeloid leukemia with 50% circulating 
      blasts and was refractory to azacitidine and CPX-351, leading to death from 
      progression. This case illustrates that GTBM and marrow fibrosis can occur 
      simultaneously in relapsed MDS after transplantation, potentially driven by 
      cytokine dysregulation related to neoplastic clones and chronic GVHD.
