PT  - Journal Article
DEP - 20260819
PL  - England
TA  - Ther Adv Hematol
JT  - Therapeutic advances in hematology
JID - 101549589
PMC - PMC13490784
OAB - Acute myeloid leukemia is a type of blood cancer that usually starts in the bone 
      marrow, where blood cells are made. Some patients have rare genetic changes that 
      may affect how the disease behaves and which treatments may work best. One of 
      these rare changes is called the EWSR1::FEV fusion. Very few patients with this 
      change have been described before, so doctors still know little about its 
      features and the best way to treat it. In this report, we describe a 45-year-old 
      man who came to the hospital with tiredness, small bleeding spots on the skin, 
      and very low blood counts. Tests showed that he had acute myeloid leukemia, but 
      his leukemia cells also showed some features that are more often seen in lymphoid 
      blood cancers. Genetic testing found the rare EWSR1::FEV fusion. Because his bone 
      marrow was weak and he had very low blood counts, we did not use a standard 
      intensive chemotherapy approach. Instead, he received a lower-intensity treatment 
      with venetoclax and azacitidine. After one treatment cycle, he achieved complete 
      remission, meaning that leukemia cells could no longer be seen by routine bone 
      marrow examination. The level of the rare genetic fusion also fell clearly after 
      treatment, and remission was maintained during follow-up treatment. This case 
      adds to the small number of published reports on this rare form of leukemia. It 
      suggests that this genetic change may define a special subgroup of leukemia with 
      mixed features. It also suggests that venetoclax-based treatment may be a useful 
      option, especially for patients who may not tolerate intensive chemotherapy well.
