IS  - 1470-2118 (Print)
IS  - 1470-2118 (Linking)
VI  - 22
IP  - 3
DP  - 2022 May
TI  - Thrombocytopenia: the good, the bad and the ugly.
PG  - 214-217
LID - 10.7861/clinmed.2022-0146 [doi]
AB  - New thrombocytopenia may be associated with a variety of conditions and diagnosis 
      can be challenging. Presentation can vary from life-threatening bleeding or 
      thrombosis to an incidental finding in an asymptomatic patient. New 
      thrombocytopenia requires urgent investigation. Investigations are mainly guided 
      by findings from the clinical history, physical examination, full blood count and 
      blood film analysis. Aside from the actively bleeding patient, rare but 
      life-threatening causes of thrombocytopenia must be identified early as they 
      require urgent treatment. These include thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, 
      disseminated intravascular coagulation, suspicion of new acute promyelocytic 
      leukaemia, and vaccine-induced prothrombotic immune thrombocytopenia. Here, we 
      discuss how to approach a patient with new thrombocytopenia, along with key 
      differentials not to be missed.
