TI  - Acute Sixth Nerve Palsy and Retinal Hemorrhages as the Presenting Features of 
      Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia.
PG  - e131-e133
LID - 10.1097/WNO.0000000000000920 [doi]
AB  - A 36-year-old apparently healthy man presented with acute onset of diplopia. 
      Examination demonstrated left sixth nerve palsy with 3 retinal hemorrhages noted 
      in one eye. Gadolinium-enhanced high-resolution skull base MRI revealed left 
      sixth nerve enhancement involving the cisternal segment. Complete blood count, 
      cerebrospinal fluid analysis, bone marrow biopsy, and flow cytometry confirmed 
      acute T-cell lymphoblastic leukemia with central nervous system involvement. This 
      case demonstrates the value of high-resolution MRI in the evaluation of cranial 
      nerve palsy in young adults and also emphasizes the importance of systemic work 
      up in these cases, particularly when retinal findings are present.
