TI  - Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia with osteolytic bone lesions: diagnostic dilemma.
LID - bcr-2018-225008 [pii]
LID - 10.1136/bcr-2018-225008 [doi]
LID - bcr2018225008
AB  - A previously healthy 37-year-old man presented with a 10-month history of 
      intractable back pain. On examination, there was tenderness to palpation along 
      lower thoracic and lumbar spine. Complete blood count showed mild anaemia but was 
      otherwise unremarkable. Imaging studies revealed compression deformities with 
      multiple osteolytic lesions involving multiple levels of the thoracic and lumbar 
      spine. Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy were performed and demonstrated blast 
      cells involving 80% of the bone marrow cellularity. Findings on flow cytometry 
      were consistent with B-lymphoblastic leukaemia. He was subsequently started on 
      hyper-CVAD (fractionated cyclophosphamide, vincristine, Adriamycin and 
      dexamethasone) induction chemotherapy.
