DP  - 2017 Sep
TI  - Gingival enlargement in a pregnant woman with acute monocytic leukaemia: a case 
      report.
PG  - 386-389
LID - 10.1111/adj.12525 [doi]
AB  - The objective of the present study was to report the case of a pregnant woman 
      with severe gingival enlargement for 3 months with undiagnosed acute leukaemia. 
      The pregnant woman presented with anaemia and generalized gingival enlargement. A 
      provisional diagnosis of gingival enlargement in pregnancy was made. Twelve days 
      after the initial treatment, the patient was referred and admitted to the 
      haematology department of a local hospital with clinical signs of anaemia and 
      thrombocytopenia. Blood count showed a white blood cell count of 9.68 x 10(9) /L, 
      with a haemoglobin count of 64.0 g/L and a platelet count of 17 x 10(9) /L. Bone 
      marrow aspiration showed 94.5% monoblasts, and the morphological diagnosis was 
      acute monocytic leukaemia. One day after admission, the patient delivered a male 
      infant by Caesarean section. Ten days after the Caesarean section, the patient 
      was started on a course of chemotherapy. Pulmonary infection, hypokalaemia, and 
      respiratory failure developed, and the patient died 23 days after the Caesarean 
      section. The present case shows the importance of awareness of severe gingival 
      enlargement as an initial oral sign of acute leukaemia.
