IS  - 1042-8194 (Print)
IS  - 1026-8022 (Linking)
VI  - 56
IP  - 11
DP  - 2015
TI  - Elevated lymphocyte count at time of acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis is 
      associated with shorter remission.
PG  - 3109-15
LID - 10.3109/10428194.2015.1020060 [doi]
AB  - In solid tumors, decreased absolute lymphocyte count (ALC) at diagnosis was found 
      to be associated with poorer outcome, but there is only limited data on the 
      impact of ALC in acute myeloid leukemia (AML). In this study we evaluated the 
      prognostic value of ALC on outcome in 259 adult patients with AML who responded 
      to induction therapy. Higher than normal ALC at diagnosis was associated with 
      shorter remission (HR 4.06; p < 0.001), and decreased relapse free and overall 
      survival (HR 3.47; p < 0.001 and HR 3.85; p < 0.001 respectively). Flow cytometry 
      showed low frequency of natural killer (NK) cells and high frequency of CD4+ T 
      cells (which includes the subset of T regulatory cells) in the high ALC group. 
      Low frequency of NK cells and potentially high frequency of inhibitory T 
      regulatory cells may result in weaker immune responses against residual leukemia 
      and may explain the poorer outcome of the high ALC group.
