OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20151228
LR  - 20261011
IS  - 1534-6269 (Electronic)
IS  - 1523-3790 (Linking)
VI  - 17
IP  - 5
DP  - 2015
TI  - Thrombosis in leukemia: incidence, causes, and practical management.
PG  - 444
LID - 10.1007/s11912-015-0444-2 [doi]
AB  - In patients with acute leukemia (AL), coagulation disorders, which include both 
      thrombotic and hemorrhagic events, are part of the clinical spectrum both at 
      diagnosis and during its evolution. The incidence of these events has been 
      reported by several authors in the range of 1-36 %. This heterogeneity appears to 
      be related to the type of patients included, the study design, and 
      patient-related factors as well as the treatment used. The pathophysiology of 
      thrombosis in AL is complex and multifactorial and includes a myriad of factors 
      that contribute to cancer procoagulant state: synthesis of procoagulant factors 
      by circulating blasts, cellular microparticles, use of endovascular devices 
      (catheters), type of chemotherapy used (L-asparaginase) to name a few. Currently, 
      treatment relies in the use of heparin, followed by vitamin K antagonists for 3 
      to 6 months. However, randomized controlled studies are required in patients with 
      AL and thrombosis to confirm its safety, duration, and effectiveness.
