TI  - A young leukemic patient with unusual catastrophic intestinal complication.
PG  - 48-54
LID - 10.4103/0377-4929.151187 [doi]
AB  - A 14-year-old child with acute lymphoblastic leukemia who had completed induction 
      chemotherapy presented with fever and diffuse musculoskeletal pains which was 
      thought to be a constellation of myositis, arthralgias and arthritis. 
      Investigations revealed initially showed normal peripheral blood counts but had 
      pancytopenia and pre-terminally blasts were seen in the peripheral blood smear. 
      He had bone marrow necrosis. Disseminated intravascular coagulation was suspected 
      with a positive fungal serology. At autopsy, he had evidence of disease relapsed 
      in lymph nodes, liver, spleen, testes and kidneys. There was extensive 
      pseudomembranous colitis and appendicitis with changes of toxic megacolon.
