TI  - Reticulin fibres anchor leukaemic blasts in the marrow of patients with acute 
      lymphoblastic leukaemia.
PG  - 333-5
LID - 10.1016/j.mehy.2011.05.007 [doi]
AB  - Reticulin fibrosis has been recognized in childhood ALL at diagnosis as part of 
      the altered stromal structure in the bone marrow (BM). Increased fibre density is 
      correlated with a higher concentration of leukaemia cells in the BM and lower 
      numbers of blasts in peripheral blood. We hypothesize that these fibres anchor 
      the leukaemia cells within the BM in close proximity to BM stromal cells (BMSC). 
      The BMSC are a rich source of growth factors and cytokines which enhance 
      leukaemia cell growth and provide protection against chemotherapy. Mobilizing the 
      cells by breaking the 'anchoring ropes' could lead to greater exposure to 
      apoptotic signals.
