VI  - 130
IP  - 8-9 Pt 1
DP  - 2003 Aug-Sep
TI  - [Aleukemic monoblastic leukemia cutis].
PG  - 773-5
AB  - BACKGROUND: The diagnostic and prognostic value of specific cutaneous lesions in 
      acute leukemia is well-known. Paradoxically, these lesions may initially develop 
      without peripheral blood or bone marrow involvement. We report the case of a 
      patient with cutaneous lesions of acute monoblastic leukemia whereas peripheral 
      blood was normal and massive infiltration of dermis was demonstrated. 
      OBSERVATION: A 49 year-old man had papules and nodules of the back and upper arms 
      evolving for several months. Histological examination with appropriate 
      immunostaining led to the diagnosis of specific cutaneous lesions of acute 
      monoblastic leukemia. Several hemograms with peripheral blood smears were normal, 
      bone marrow smear demonstrated an important blastic infiltration on one site and 
      a discrete infiltration on another. Cutaneous lesions disappeared with 
      chemotherapy. DISCUSSION: Specific cutaneous lesions may be isolated during acute 
      leukemia, and called aleukemic leukemia cutis. These are a rare form, the 
      underlying mechanism of which relies on the accumulation of small quantities of 
      myeloblasts in bone narrow and with high tropism for the dermis.
