DP  - 1998 Sep-Oct
TI  - The limping child: a manifestation of acute leukemia.
PG  - 625-9
AB  - Nine patients who presented to our institution with the chief complaint of a limp 
      and no history of trauma were subsequently diagnosed with leukemia. A review of 
      these patients identified clinical and laboratory findings that helped to 
      establish the diagnosis. The presence of an antalgic gait with complaints of pain 
      of variable intensity and duration, an irritable hip or knee, a mild to moderate 
      elevation in body temperature, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, an increased 
      erythrocyte sedimentation rate, thrombocytopenia, anemia, decreased neutrophils, 
      increased lymphocytes, or blast cells on the peripheral blood smear should cause 
      the physician to suspect leukemia in a limping child. Bone marrow biopsy confirms 
      the diagnosis.
