TI  - Serum transferrin receptor and erythropoiesis in children with newly diagnosed 
      acute lymphoblastic leukemia.
PG  - 823-8
AB  - Thirty-five children with acute lymphoblastic leukemia were monitored weekly 
      during the first 12 weeks of chemotherapy. The transferrin receptor (TfR) 
      concentration was 2.8 +/- 0.2 mg/l (mean +/- S.E.M.) at diagnosis, decreased up 
      to 3 weeks, and then increased reaching a maximal level at 8 weeks. The mean 
      values for reticulocyte counts followed a similar pattern. In contrast, serum 
      erythropoietin and ferritin levels were generally high. Those patients whose 
      erythropoiesis was more accelerated had higher serum TfR concentrations. We 
      conclude that among these patients the TfR level reflected the rate of 
      erythropoiesis and was independent of the level of erythropoietin.
