VI  - 39
IP  - 5
DP  - 1991 May
TI  - Chronic myelomonocytic leukaemia (an analysis of fourteen consecutive cases).
PG  - 390-3
AB  - Fourteen consecutive cases of chronic myelomonocytic leukaemia aged 6 to 73 (mean 
      40.5) years were reviewed to define the natural history of the disease and the 
      risk of acute transformation. The common presenting features included anaemia, 
      fever, purpura, and bleeding tendencies. Abnormal karyotypes were seen in 4 of 6 
      patients subjected to cytogenetic analysis. Low dose cytosine arabinoside 
      achieved complete remission in two and partial remission in one, of the four 
      patients treated with this modality. The mean survival was 5.6 (range 2-12) 
      months and two patients) evolved to acute myeloid leukaemia. The long term 
      survival with the present form of therapy in chronic myelomonocytic leukaemia is 
      poor.
