DP  - 1989 Sep
TI  - Neurological manifestations as the initial presentation of acute myelogenous 
      leukemia.
PG  - 481-4
AB  - This case report illustrates neurological deficits as an unusual presentation of 
      acute myelogenous leukemia. Neurological deficits are rare early in this disease. 
      Our patient presented with anorexia, malaise, headache, and multiple cranial 
      nerve palsies. A high WBC count and abnormal peripheral smear led to the 
      diagnosis of leukemia. This report demonstrates that, although rare, CNS symptoms 
      may be the initial manifestation of leukemia. Blood dyscrasias should not be 
      overlooked in patients with the acute onset of neurological symptoms. A complete 
      blood count and differential should be obtained under those circumstances.
