DP  - 1986
TI  - United Kingdom Medical Research Council Acute Lymphoblastic Leukaemia (UKALL) 
      Trials I-VIII: clinical features and results of treatment in four groups of 
      children with adverse prognostic features.
PG  - 182-6
AB  - Four groups of children with poor-prognosis disease were selected for study from 
      the Medical Research Council childhood ALL trials. Each group was reviewed for 
      distinguishing clinical features and response to therapy. The four comprised 
      those with a diagnostic white cell count over 50 X 10(9)/l, those with a 
      mediastinal mass, those less than a year old at diagnosis, and those presenting 
      with meningeal involvement. They accounted for 17, 5.3, 2.6, and 3%, 
      respectively, of unselected children with ALL. As expected, all four categories 
      showed significantly inferior relapse-free survival to the rest, but only in 
      patients with a mediastinal mass and in the under-1-year-olds did a recognisable 
      syndrome emerge clearly associated with other clinical features. These two 
      syndromes, high-count T-ALL and high-count infant null-ALL, are logical 
      candidates for alternative therapy. Established meningeal disease also continues 
      to present a major therapeutic challenge.
