VI  - 7
IP  - 1
DP  - 1985 Spring
TI  - Cytologic diagnosis of the acute nonlymphoid leukemias. I. Morphologic, 
      cytochemical, and ultrastructural features.
PG  - 21-44
AB  - The cytologic evaluation of a case of acute leukemia proceeds in two stages: 1) 
      assigning the leukemia to one of two major classes, acute lymphocytic leukemia 
      (ALL) or acute nonlymphocytic leukemia (ANLL); and 2) determining the proper 
      subclassification of ALL or ANLL to which the case belongs. Although features 
      such as nuclear morphology, number and nature of nucleoli, and 
      nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio are useful criteria, the major morphologic features 
      which distinguish ANLL cells from ALL cells are the presence of azurophilic 
      granules and/or inclusions derived from fusion of these granules (Auer bodies). 
      In most instances these can be visualized in the conventional blood film or bone 
      marrow preparation. Sometimes, however, granules and Auer bodies are too small or 
      too few to be seen with the light microscope. In such cases histochemical or 
      ultrastructural studies will aid in proper classification.
